      "text": "Start by calculating the number of favorable outcomes. There are 4 sixes and 4 queens of the same suit. Therefore, if both cards are drawn at random, one of the sixes or one of the Queens has to be chosen. The question is asking to find how many times each of the sixes or queens occur in the two-digit count. The number of ways to arrange something can be expressed using denominators. The denominator of the first six is $\\dbinom{4}{1}\\cdot\\dbinom{4}{1}$ because we want to choose the six first and then the Queen. Similarly, for the Queen, the denominator is also $\\dbinom{4}{1}\\cdot\\dbinom{4}{1}$. Because the sixes and Queens are chosen independently, the answer is the product of the two denominators. Since these two ways of thinking give us the same answer, I will simplify and divide as follows: $\\dfrac{52 \\cdot 51}{2}$ then the number of ways of choosing two cards is: $\\dbinom{52}{2}$. Simplifying: $\\dfrac{52 \\cdot 51}{2\\cdot 51}$. Therefore, the numerator is option D.",
